IMPORT prelude

-- domain.l4: the shared nouns of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, written once by the SURVEYOR (PHASE1/PLAN.md section 8 says why each is here).
-- Every group module IMPORTs this file and never edits it; a noun an encoder needs that is missing goes in their FRAGMENT as a "nouns request".
-- It holds only nouns that two or more groups use: the text vintages and offices of the Minister, the units of the Law (for commencement), the kinds of entity of
-- Article 2(1), the purposes and their heads under Article 6(1), the Entity and Registered charity records that the Parts after Article 2 take as input, the roles that act,
-- the notice, ground and decision enumerations that one group decides and another consumes, and the penalty data of the offences.
-- No rules and no #EVAL live here: whatever depends on a reading of the Law belongs to a group, and every discretion ("opinion", "satisfied", "prescribed") is an input.
-- The raw witness-level facts that only G1 reads (which sub-paragraphs of Article 2(1) a body fits) belong to a G1 record; Entity below is the entity AS ARTICLE 2 LEAVES IT.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. Time: which text of the Law, and which units are in force (decided by G7; consumed by G1, G9 and the integrator)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The nine point-in-time versions of the consolidated text (Table of Legislation History).
-- A day before 1 January 2019 has no consolidated version among the sources: the first constructor exists so that a rule can name that case and REFUSE on it.
-- The version of 27 February 2024 begins at 9.30 a.m. (R&O.10/2024), so a vintage of a DATE alone is not defined on that day.
DECLARE `Vintage of the text` IS ONE OF
    `before the first consolidated version`
    `the text of 1 January 2019`
    `the text of 31 May 2019`
    `the text of 16 December 2019`
    `the text of 1 January 2020`
    `the text of 2 March 2021`
    `the text of 12 April 2023`
    `the text of 27 February 2024`
    `the text of 16 October 2025`
    `the text of 2 October 2026`

-- The office that the definition "Minister" (Article 1) names, text by text.
DECLARE `Office of the Minister` IS ONE OF
    `the Chief Minister`
    `the Minister for External Relations`
    `the Minister for External Relations and Financial Services`

-- A unit of the Law, for the staged commencement. The Table of Legislation History speaks in the ORIGINAL numbers (it names Articles 42, 43 and 44);
-- the present Articles 41, 42 and 43 were the original 42, 43 and 44(1), and the original 41 and 44(2) and (3) are spent (Table of Renumbered Provisions).
DECLARE `Unit of the Law` IS ONE OF
    `a Part of the Law` HAS `part number` IS A NUMBER @desc Part number, 1 to 9
    `an Article of the Law as now numbered` HAS `article number` IS A NUMBER @desc Article number in the consolidated text, 1 to 43
    `an Article of the Law as originally numbered` HAS `original article number` IS A NUMBER @desc Article number before the renumbering, 1 to 44
    `a Schedule to the Law` HAS `schedule number` IS A NUMBER @desc Schedule number, 1 or 2

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. Who and what (Articles 1 and 2; decided by G1)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The sub-paragraphs of Article 2(1). An entity may fall within more than one (Article 8(3)(a)(i) asks "whether it also falls within sub-paragraph (a)"),
-- so an entity has a LIST of these. Sub-paragraph (ba) exists only in the text of 16 October 2025 and later.
DECLARE `Kind of entity` IS ONE OF
    `the trustees of a trust`
    `the fidéicommissaires of a court-approved fidéicommis`
    `a body corporate that is an NPO established under the law of Jersey`
    `an incorporated 1862 association`
    `a foundation`
    `a body corporate established by an enactment, an Act of the States or a Royal Charter`
    `a company`
    `a body incorporated under, but not by, an enactment other than the Companies (Jersey) Law 1991`
    `a body incorporated under, but not by, an Act of the United Kingdom Parliament or Order in Council`
    `an unincorporated body or association of persons`
    `an entity substantially similar to one of the above, established under the law of another jurisdiction`
    `an entity added by an Order under Article 2(2)` HAS `sub-paragraph added` IS A STRING @desc The label of the sub-paragraph the Order adds to Article 2(1)

-- Where an entity stands on the register on a day. "Registered charity" (Article 1) means an entity entered in the general or the restricted section.
-- The standing is an input: the register is the Commissioner's record, and the rules of Parts 4 and 5 are acts that change it, not computations of it.
DECLARE `Standing on the register` IS ONE OF
    `not on the register`
    `an applicant for registration`
    `entered in the general section`
    `entered in the restricted section`
    `formerly registered and entered in the historic section`

-- The roles that act, are acted upon or decide in the Law (the PARTY of a regulative rule). Identity within a role (which person is the Commissioner,
-- which Minister holds the office) is a separate fact; see Office of the Minister and the Schedule 1 paragraph 4(5) rule.
DECLARE `Legal actor` IS ONE OF
    `the Commissioner`
    `a member of the Commissioner's staff`
    `the Minister`
    `the States`
    `the court`
    `the Attorney General`
    `the Bailiff`
    `the Charity Tribunal`
    `the Jersey Financial Services Commission`
    `the Comptroller`
    `an applicant for registration`
    `a registered charity`
    `a governor`
    `any person`

-- The eight descriptions in Article 19(1) of a person in relation to whom there is a reportable matter.
DECLARE `Kind of reportable matter` IS ONE OF
    `misconduct as a governor that led to a required steps notice`
    `a disqualification order or other restriction on acting as a governor imposed under this Law`
    `disqualification as a charity trustee under the law of the United Kingdom or an equivalent position elsewhere`
    `disqualification as a company director, or an equivalent disqualification outside Jersey`
    `bankruptcy or other insolvency`
    `a conviction, whether or not spent, for an offence under this Law`
    `an unspent conviction for an offence falling within Article 2(10)(b)`
    `a description prescribed by Order`

-- A governor of an entity (Article 2(7)), as a record of the facts about the person that other groups need.
-- Whether a person IS a governor is G1's rule; downstream groups receive a list of the people who are.
DECLARE Governor HAS
    name IS A STRING @desc The governor's name as it would be entered on the register under Article 8(3)(b)
    `is a natural person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc FALSE where the governor is a company or other body, for example a corporate trustee
    `is resident in Jersey` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a natural person governor is resident in Jersey
    `is itself a Jersey entity by virtue of Article 2(3)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the governor is itself an entity within sub-paragraphs (a) to (h) of Article 2(1)
    `reportable matters` IS A LIST OF `Kind of reportable matter` @desc Every kind of reportable matter that currently describes this person; empty if none

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. The register and the Commissioner's decisions (Parts 4 and 5; decided by G3 to G6, consumed across the groups)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The sixteen heads of Article 6(1), plus the heads that Regulations under Article 6(3) may add. A purpose either falls under a head or does not;
-- which head is a judgement ("may reasonably be regarded as analogous" in (p) is an input), so a head is a field of Purpose, never computed from the words of a purpose.
DECLARE `Head of charitable purpose` IS ONE OF
    `the prevention or relief of poverty`
    `the advancement of education`
    `the advancement of religion`
    `the advancement of health`
    `the saving of lives`
    `the advancement of citizenship or community development`
    `the advancement of the arts, heritage, culture or science`
    `the advancement of public participation in sport`
    `the provision of recreational facilities or the organisation of recreational activities`
    `the advancement of human rights, conflict resolution or reconciliation`
    `the promotion of religious or racial harmony`
    `the promotion of equality and diversity`
    `the advancement of environmental protection or improvement`
    `the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantage`
    `the advancement of animal welfare`
    `any other purpose that may reasonably be regarded as analogous to one of the above`
    `a purpose added by Regulations under Article 6(3)` HAS `label of the added sub-paragraph` IS A STRING @desc The label the Regulations give the added sub-paragraph of Article 6(1)

-- One purpose of an entity (Article 2(6)). Article 5(1)(a) quantifies over all of an entity's purposes, so the entity carries a LIST of these.
DECLARE Purpose HAS
    description IS A STRING @desc The purpose in the words of the entity's constitution or registered statement
    head IS A MAYBE `Head of charitable purpose` @desc The head of Article 6(1) the purpose falls under, if any; NOTHING if it falls under none
    `is purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose of the entity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Article 5(1)(a)(ii)
    `is advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Article 6(5): then neither a charitable purpose nor an ancillary one
    `is advancing a philosophical belief` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Article 6(2)(f), whether or not the belief involves belief in a god

-- The entity as Article 2 leaves it: what the Parts that follow read about it. G1 derives kinds of entity, Jersey-entity status and governors from the raw facts of Article 2;
-- every other group takes this record as one GIVEN (or the single field it needs as the standard GIVEN of PLAN section (c)).
DECLARE Entity HAS
    name IS A STRING @desc The entity's name; the registered name once it is registered
    `standing on the register` IS A `Standing on the register` @desc Where the entity is entered on the day in question
    `kinds of entity` IS A LIST OF `Kind of entity` @desc The sub-paragraphs of Article 2(1) within which the entity falls; empty if it is not an entity
    `is a Jersey entity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is a Jersey entity under Article 2(3)
    governors IS A LIST OF Governor @desc The governors on the day in question, each a person who is a governor under Article 2(7)
    purposes IS A LIST OF Purpose @desc All of the entity's purposes (Article 2(6))

-- What the register holds about an entity that is, or has been, registered (Article 8(3) and (5), Article 12(2)). Only the fields that two or more groups read are here;
-- fields only G3 reads (addresses, other premises, the annual-return note) belong to a G3 record that wraps this one.
DECLARE `Registered charity` HAS
    entity IS AN Entity @desc The entity; its standing says whether it is registered in the general or restricted section, or formerly registered
    `registration number` IS A STRING @desc The number allocated under Article 12(2)(b)
    `registered name` IS A STRING @desc The registered name entered under Article 12(2)(a)
    `alternative registered names` IS A LIST OF STRING @desc Further names entered under Article 12(5); empty if none
    `date registered` IS A DATE @desc The date on which the charity was registered (Article 8(3)(g))
    `date deregistered` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc NOTHING while the entity is registered; the effective date of deregistration otherwise
    `registered charitable purposes` IS A LIST OF STRING @desc The purposes in the statement approved under Article 8(3)(e)
    `registered public benefit statement` IS A STRING @desc The statement approved under Article 8(3)(f)
    `is an organized religious charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the charity is categorized as one under an Order under Article 28

-- What "year" means in Part 4. Articles 8(3)(j), 13(7) and 13(9)(a) say "the most recent year" and "the year to which the return relates" without defining it,
-- while Schedule 1 paragraph 4(8) says "calendar year" when it means one. G3 and G5 each carry a fork on it (F3.2, F5.2); both type their field with THIS enumeration
-- so that the integrator can set the two fields from one reading. The reading each group takes by default is the calendar year.
DECLARE `Meaning of the year of an annual return` IS ONE OF
    `the calendar year`
    `the charity's own financial year`
    `the twelve months from each anniversary of registration`

-- The five grounds on which the Commissioner may serve a required steps notice (Article 27(1)(a) to (e)).
DECLARE `Belief that prompts a required steps notice` IS ONE OF
    `there has been misconduct by a registered charity`
    `a registered charity does not pass the charity test`
    `a governor of a registered charity has engaged in misconduct`
    `a governor of a registered charity is unfit by reason of a reportable matter`
    `a registered charity has ceased to meet the requirements of Article 11(4)(c) or (d)`

DECLARE `Recipient of a required steps notice` IS ONE OF
    `the registered charity`
    `a governor of the registered charity`

-- A required steps notice (Article 27). The steps themselves are the Commissioner's opinion and are not modelled; what other groups read is below.
DECLARE `Required steps notice` HAS
    `served on` IS A `Recipient of a required steps notice` @desc Whom the notice was served on
    `prompted by` IS A `Belief that prompts a required steps notice` @desc The belief under Article 27(1) that the notice rests on
    `date served` IS A DATE @desc The date on which the notice was served (the Law does not say how service is fixed; see Article 38)
    `steps to be taken by` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The end of the time the notice specifies for the steps, if it specifies one
    `requires a governor to be suspended, removed or replaced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Article 27(4); to that extent invalid for an organized religious charity (Article 28(3))
    `has been complied with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the steps required have been taken

-- Why an entity was deregistered (Articles 15 and 16), as the historic-section entry and the appeal right both need it (Articles 8(5)(c), 33(2)(a)).
DECLARE `Basis of deregistration` IS ONE OF
    `on the entity's own application under Article 15`
    `the entity no longer meets the charity test`
    `the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity`
    `the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`
    `a circumstance prescribed by Order under Article 16(1)(d)`
    `the entity no longer exists`

-- The decisions of the Commissioner that other provisions refer to: those Schedule 1 paragraph 4(3) reserves from delegation to staff (the decision WHETHER, so either
-- outcome), and those Article 33 makes appealable (a particular outcome: "to refuse to register", "to deregister"). A decision is therefore a subject and an outcome.
DECLARE `Subject of a decision of the Commissioner` IS ONE OF
    `whether to register an applicant, Article 11`
    `whether to enter a charity in the restricted section, Article 9(6) and (7)`
    `whether to permit a change or addition of registered name, Article 12(4)`
    `whether to require a charity to apply to change its registered name, Article 12(6)`
    `whether to approve a proposed amendment of the charity's purposes, Article 13(3)`
    `whether to approve a proposed amendment of the registered public benefit statement, Article 13(4)(b)`
    `whether to deregister a charity on its own application, Article 15(3)`
    `whether to deregister a charity in other cases, Article 16`
    `whether to impose a requirement contained in a notice under Article 26`
    `whether to impose a requirement contained in a required steps notice`
    `any other matter`

DECLARE `Outcome of a decision of the Commissioner` IS ONE OF
    `the Commissioner does what was sought or proposed`
    `the Commissioner refuses`

DECLARE `Decision of the Commissioner` HAS
    subject IS A `Subject of a decision of the Commissioner` @desc Which decision of the Commissioner is meant
    outcome IS AN `Outcome of a decision of the Commissioner` @desc Whether the Commissioner did what was sought or proposed, or refused

-- The orders the court may make over a registered charity (Article 14(3)(a) to (i)), which Article 17(9) extends to a deregistered entity.
-- The order's content (which name, which governor, which conditions) is an input and is not modelled.
DECLARE `Order of the court over a charity` IS ONE OF
    `an order prohibiting the charity or a governor from holding out as a charity, soliciting donations or another specified action`
    `an order appointing a person to manage the affairs of the charity in place of its governors`
    `an order appointing a person to be a governor`
    `an order suspending or removing a governor or another person concerned in the management`
    `an order requiring a holder of property not to part with it without the court's consent`
    `an order restricting transactions or payments without the court's consent`
    `an order subjecting the administration to supervision, restraint or conditions`
    `an order requiring a person to take steps to remedy the effects of misconduct`
    `an order making ancillary provision`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4. Penalties (offences in Articles 19, 20, 21, 23, 26, 29; Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(f); Article 37 carries the penalty to an officer)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The Law never states a maximum fine in money. It says "a fine" (no maximum stated) or "a fine of level 3 on the standard scale";
-- the amount of level 3 is in the Criminal Justice (Standard Scale of Fines) (Jersey) Law, which is not among the sources, so it is not modelled.
DECLARE `Fine provided` IS ONE OF
    `no fine is provided`
    `a fine with no maximum stated in the Law`
    `a fine of a level on the standard scale` HAS level IS A NUMBER @desc The level on the standard scale

-- In what unit a term of imprisonment is measured. The Law writes "one year", "2 years", "3 months"; G6 (F6.16), G7 (F7.8), G8 (F8.8) and G9 (F9.13) each carry a fork on it
-- and type their field with THIS enumeration so that the integrator sets all four from one reading. The reading each group takes by default is calendar months.
DECLARE `Unit of a term of imprisonment` IS ONE OF
    `calendar months`
    `days`

-- The penalty the Law attaches to an offence. A term of imprisonment is a MAXIMUM, stored in months ("one year" is 12, "2 years" is 24); see `Unit of a term of imprisonment`.
DECLARE `Penalty provided` HAS
    `imprisonment up to, in months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc NOTHING where the Law provides no imprisonment; otherwise the term in months
    `fine` IS A `Fine provided` @desc The fine, if any
